            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance news is that NASA’s rover captured a first-of-its-kind observation from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. The image sequence was taken by Perseverance’s Mastcam-Z camera on July 2, 2026, and NASA/JPL published the story on Aug. 5, 2026. NASA describes it as the first time Earth has been seen “vanishing” behind another object from the surface of another planet【5:1†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates include:\n\n- **New “Image of the Week”**: NASA’s raw-images site featured a Perseverance “Image of the Week” for mission Week 285, covering July 26–Aug. 1, 2026【5:0†source】.\n- **Jezero Crater rim science**: In July 2026, NASA reported that Perseverance studied the “Broom Point member,” a roughly 245-foot-thick stack of ancient rock on Jezero Crater’s rim that appears to record repeated asteroid impacts【5:2†source】.\n- **Mission status**: Perseverance remains an active mission, continuing to explore Jezero Crater, search for signs of ancient microbial life, and collect rock/regolith samples for possible return to Earth by a future mission【5:4†source】.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-watches-earth-vanish-behind-martian-moon/",
